# G01 — Baseline And Target Matrix

Project: PowerPC Darwin OS personality for hyper-linux.app  
Status: proposed; implementation and tests not yet performed  
Owner: hl + ppc32rt  
Dependencies: none

## Objective
Inventory existing hl iOS Mach-O/Mach native/wine modes, X11, Android UI, ppc32rt boundaries and supported Mac OS X 10.4/10.5 PPC32 ABIs.

## Implementation contract
- Existing `ppc32rt` stays tiny; `hl` owns guest Mach-O, dyld, Mach/BSD personality, framework resolution and application lifecycle. Reuse iOS implementation after code inspection; never assume ABI identity.
- Preserve existing working Android, iOS, Linux/X11 and Linux PowerPC paths; establish baseline tests before editing shared code.
- Treat guest pointers, strings, handles, Mach messages, files, graphics resources and executable metadata as untrusted; validate bounds, lifetime and ownership.
- Keep `ppc32rt` small: ISA, CPU state, interpreter, AArch64 DBT, guest memory and narrow traps only; put Darwin OS and UI behavior in `hyper-linux.app` (`hl`).
- Support both wine-style compatibility/stub-library mode and native mode using a user-supplied, legally obtained historical Mac OS X framework/system bundle; never ship proprietary system libraries.
- Use real fixtures and distinguish observed behavior from unimplemented assumptions. Preserve host/guest page-size and endian separation.

## Deliverables
Compatibility matrix, reusable component map and fixture provenance.

## Acceptance gate
Known-good hl and ppc32rt tests reproduced; target release/ABI selected.
- Add positive, negative, malformed-input, fault and regression coverage appropriate to this goal; include exact commands, fixture provenance, host/guest versions and observed results.
- Record dependencies, blockers, reviewed code, and reproducible evidence in the goal closeout. No unsupported success claims.

## Boundaries and risks
Historical Darwin PPC trap and framework ABIs differ from iOS; guest kernel and service expectations remain difficult. System bundle licensing and provenance must be respected.

## Closeout checklist
- [ ] Implementation reviewed and committed
- [ ] Focused tests and regressions executed with recorded results
- [ ] Integration behavior verified in `hl` where applicable
- [ ] Limitations, unsupported cases and security concerns documented
